<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#serfdom">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Serfdom</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Serfdom was a form of unfree labour in which peasants were bound to the land and owed labour or dues to a lord. It was widespread in medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and lasted longest in central and eastern Europe. It features in the profiles of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>, where it intensified with grain exports, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, where it was abolished in 1861.</p></div>
